+
+Use the 509-element basis as a normal-form engine; do not prepend its 405,524 terms to an ordinary F4 solve. Accept a quotient point only after reconstructing every eliminated and dormant coordinate and checking every pristine D23 and Row-24 row.
+
+Required gates are exact source/GB/order/fiber hashes; affine, rank, and chart-unit certificates; absence of removed variables; raw-versus-quotient random evaluation; backsolve at all six witnesses; replay of every pristine Row-24 row; τ and all \(h\)-sign branches; \(W\)-chart saturation; the `+42` mutation; cross-prime support; and every rank boundary.
+
+### 3.3 Size, runtime, and stopping rules
+
+**MEASURED:** \(G_{23}\) loads all 405,524 terms into FLINT in 1.95 seconds with 105.4 MiB peak RSS. The D23 raw build took 5,587 seconds; its raw Row-22 block has 35,988 terms, while the rejected “substitute before Schur” route reached 4,971,007 terms.
+
+**ESTIMATE:** pristine Row 24 will contain roughly 40,000–200,000 terms; the full control should be about 100,000–260,000 terms or 2–10 MB. The existing nonincremental Python builder may take 1–4 hours; a slot-incremental cached series builder should take 5–30 minutes. Once the raw row exists, Schur plus coefficient normal forms should take seconds to a few minutes and stay below 1 GB if the reduced block stays under 100,000 terms. Warn at 100,000 terms or degree 16; stop expansion at 250,000 terms and retain the hybrid/DAG representation.
